              "<div>Most nursing programs do have some form of an exit exam, though no one really talks about it until you are already halfway through your final semester. It feels like the last big checkpoint before everything shifts toward the real world. Many schools use tools like the ATI Exit Exam or the HESI Exit Exam to check whether you are thinking like someone who is almost ready to step into practice. You might notice that the questions have a very different feel from the ones you see in the first year. They are heavier, more layered, and they ask you to make safe choices even when the situation feels a little messy. Some campuses require it for RN students, some for LPN students, and some use it simply as a readiness measure rather than a pass or fail moment. Either way, it is meant to help you breathe easier when you finally face the NCLEX.</div>"
